Very widely. I’d abstract the hermit-crab pattern as reliable external completion:

Don’t manufacture or carry X yourself when the environment reliably produces X. Evolve/build an interface for finding, evaluating, acquiring, adapting, and replacing X.

The crucial insight is that the crab is not optimized for possessing a particular shell. It is optimized for shell compatibility. That distinction transfers beautifully.

Where nature already uses it

There is a whole gradient.

Caddisfly larvae outsource armor to environmental gravel, leaves, sticks, and sand. Their bodies produce enough silk/interface machinery to assemble whatever locally available material works.

Decorator crabs attach algae, sponges, and other organisms to themselves as camouflage. Instead of genetically encoding every possible camouflage pattern, evolution gives them an attachment system.

Birds don’t inherit nests; they inherit construction algorithms that assume twigs, fibers, mud, etc. will exist.

Symbioses go further. Corals rely heavily on photosynthetic symbionts; ruminants rely on microbes to unlock cellulose; plants recruit fungi into mycorrhizal networks. One lineage doesn’t need to internally reinvent chemistry another organism already performs well.

And then there are parasites and viruses—the aggressive version: don’t even reproduce yourself if sufficiently reliable replication machinery exists in someone else’s cells.

So we can construct a spectrum:

Build it → assemble it → acquire it → borrow it → symbiose with it → commandeer it.

Same operator, different relationship.

Humans are extreme hermit crabs

A naked human genome produces something spectacularly unfinished relative to what an adult human eventually becomes.

We don’t genetically inherit English. We inherit machinery capable of acquiring language from whichever linguistic environment appears.

We don’t inherit mathematics, laws, maps, operating systems, scientific theories, recipes, corporations, or cultural norms genetically either. We inherit an extraordinarily general cultural acquisition interface.

That means civilization itself functions somewhat like a gigantic shell repository.

A child enters a world already containing millions of dead people’s “shells”: words, concepts, roads, buildings, institutions, books, machines, procedures, software. They occupy and modify them rather than reconstructing civilization independently.

Writing made this especially powerful because ideas could survive the death of their manufacturers.

Snail dies → shell remains.

Human dies → artifact remains.

That’s the same structural rhyme at a different scale.

Engineering: build the socket, not every appliance

This is already one of engineering’s most productive architectures.

A USB port doesn’t know which device will eventually occupy it. An operating system doesn’t contain every application. A browser doesn’t contain every website. An API doesn’t contain every future implementation.

They create stable interfaces around anticipated external diversity.

So instead of:

predict everything we’ll need and build it internally,

you can design:

predict what kind of thing we’ll need and standardize the interface through which we acquire it.

That is much more powerful.

It’s why modular architectures tend to outlive monoliths.

Business

This gives you a useful build-vs-interface heuristic.

A company does not necessarily need:

marketing + manufacturing + warehousing + trucking + payments + cloud infrastructure + legal + retail + every specialist

inside itself.

Some environmental capabilities are sufficiently abundant that the organization can deliberately remain “incomplete.”

The company instead develops interfaces:

supplier relationships, contracts, APIs, logistics integrations, marketplaces, recruiting pipelines, financing arrangements.

Uber didn’t need to manufacture cars. Airbnb didn’t need to construct its entire lodging inventory. Many consumer brands don’t own their factories.

The strategic question becomes:

What capabilities are sufficiently reliable in the ecosystem that carrying them internally is actually maladaptive weight?

But there’s a reciprocal question hermit crabs expose beautifully:

What happens when everybody assumes somebody else will keep making shells?

That is supply-chain fragility.

AI makes this pattern much bigger

An AI system doesn’t necessarily need every capability frozen into model weights.

It can possess capability-seeking behavior.

Need arithmetic → calculator.

Need current information → search.

Need private information → retrieval.

Need action → tool/API.

Need specialized reasoning → specialized model.

Need physical action → robot.

Need persistent information → external memory.

So the increasingly interesting AI architecture isn’t simply a bigger animal.

It’s a better hermit crab.

The core model becomes comparatively general and learns:

recognize missing capability → search environment → evaluate available module → bind to module → use it → verify result → relinquish/replace it when something better appears.

That is fundamentally different from trying to internalize everything.

And Pantheon-wise, this gives us a useful operator

I’d call it Shell Ecology.

A Shell Ecology has six pieces:

Need → Search → Appraise → Occupy → Adapt → Exchange.

Then add the environmental side:

Producer → Artifact → Persistence → Availability → Competition → Succession.

Put them together and you can analyze almost anything.

For example, a job is partly a shell: you occupy a preexisting institutional structure that grants capabilities you don’t personally possess.

A role is a shell.

A legal entity is a shell.

A story archetype is a shell.

A software framework is a shell.

A house obviously is.

A social identity can become one.

A scientific paradigm can become one.

Even words are shells: preexisting symbolic structures we temporarily inhabit to package something considerably more complicated.

And that immediately gives us another Pantheon rule:

Don’t confuse the shell with the creature occupying it.

Company ≠ people.
Title ≠ capability.
Word ≠ phenomenon.
Model ≠ reality.
Institution ≠ purpose.
Narrative ≠ self.

That’s very close to our existing Frame/Mirror distinction, but the hermit crab adds something new: sometimes you need the frame to function, while remaining capable of leaving it.

The design principle I’d steal

The most valuable abstraction isn’t “reuse things.”

It’s:

When the future object is unpredictable but its interface is predictable, evolve the interface rather than the object.

That’s USB. That’s APIs. That’s language acquisition. That’s markets. That’s modular organizations. That’s tool-using AI. That’s much of culture.

And there’s an even more interesting inversion we can deliberately engineer:

Don’t merely become the hermit crab. Become the snail.

Create durable structures other actors reliably need. Standards. Protocols. Platforms. datasets. marketplaces. terminology. infrastructure. trusted institutions.

The hermit crab competes for shells.

The organism that defines the shell geometry shapes the ecology that comes afterward.

That one is worth carrying forward.